  "explanation": {
    "short": "æ is like the \"a\" in \"cat\" (vær). gj is said like English \"y\" (gjerne is YER-neh). After n, l or r a final d is often silent (bord sounds like \"boor\", kaldt like \"kalt\"), and the t in det is silent (it sounds like \"deh\"). Days of the week start with a small letter: søndag.",
    "more": "Norwegian words also come with one of two melodies (tone 1 or tone 2), which can tell two words that are spelled alike apart. Go does not teach the melodies here: you will hear them in the recordings. Languages and nationalities also start with a small letter (norsk); only names such as Anna and Gud begin with a capital letter."
  },
